37-e3c2335ce819" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] ["question-31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =7.4 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.617\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.617,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."] ["question-31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/id #uuid "30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810"] ["answer-4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" :answer/id #uuid "4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c"] ["answer-4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" :answer/value "0.581"] ["answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/id #uuid "22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279"] ["answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/value "0.617"] ["answer-6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" :answer/id #uuid "6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3"] ["answer-6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" :answer/value "0.714"] ["answer-d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" :answer/id #uuid "d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6"] ["answer-d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" :answer/value "0.819"] ["answer-18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe" :answer/id #uuid "18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe"] ["answer-18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe" :answer/value "0.891"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/correct "answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/choices "answer-4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/choices "answer-22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/choices "answer-6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/choices "answer-d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6"] ["answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/choices "answer-18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe"] ["question-31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810"] [17592186162116 :knowledge-point/questions "question-31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819"] ["question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/id #uuid "4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d"] ["question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159939"] ["question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] ["question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\theta } & =15.8 \\\\ 5 & =15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.316,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."] ["question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/id #uuid "34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79"] ["answer-5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" :answer/id #uuid "5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2"] ["answer-5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" :answer/value "0.208"] ["answer-56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" :answer/id #uuid "56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef"] ["answer-56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" :answer/value "0.213"] ["answer-8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" :answer/id #uuid "8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde"] ["answer-8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" :answer/value "0.276"] ["answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/id #uuid "cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756"] ["answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/value "0.316"] ["answer-aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4" :answer/id #uuid "aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4"] ["answer-aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4" :answer/value "0.412"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/correct "answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/choices "answer-5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/choices "answer-56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/choices "answer-8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/choices "answer-cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756"] ["answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/choices "answer-aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4"] ["question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79"] [17592186162116 :knowledge-point/questions "question-4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d"] [17592186163265 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, X_3$ drawn from a population where $X_i\\sim B(5,\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown probability of success. Let's assume we conducted our sample and got the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the log-likelihood function for this sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln  125 + 6 \\ln \\theta +9\\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to form an estimate for the unknown parameter $\\theta.$ To do this, we find the value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the probability of getting the observed sample data. In other words, we need to maximize the likelihood function $L(\\theta).$ And, since the natural logarithm is an increasing function, a value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ will also maximize the log-likelihood function $l(\\theta).$\n\nEstimates of an unknown parameter $\\theta$ obtained by maximizing the likelihood function (or, equivalently, the log-likelihood function) are called **maximum likelihood estimates**. We'll use the usual $\\hat{\\theta}$ notation to denote a maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta.$ \n\n![](images/00/005367bc78937c5bd5b684cb260aff951de2e85d7c2907bb6e11c312ad4f24bd.png)\n\nTo find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6(\\theta -1)+9\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 6(\\theta -1)+9\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 15\\theta -6 & =0 \\\\ 15\\theta  & =6 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{6}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{6}{15}.$ \n\n**Note**: It's easy to check that this is a maximum of $l(\\theta)$ by calculating the second derivative. We'll omit this step."] [17592186163361 :tutorial/content "Let's derive a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimator for the unknown parameter $\\theta$ for a set of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables.\n\nSuppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{n}$ is an I.I.D random sample, where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n},$ the log-likelihood function is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = S\\ln\\theta + (n-S)\\ln(1-\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i$ is the number of successes in the sample. \n\nTo compute a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimate, we differentiate $l(\\theta),$ set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{S(\\theta -1)+(n-S)\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ S(\\theta -1)+(n-S)\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\cancel{S\\theta }-S+n\\theta -\\cancel{S\\theta } & =0 \\\\ n\\theta -S & =0 \\\\ n\\theta  & =S \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{S}{n} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i,\n$$\n\nwhich is simply the *mean of the sample*. \n\nThe maximum likelihood estimate for other common distributions can be derived similarly. Let's summarize the most important cases."] [17592186163362 :tutorial/content "The table below summarizes the maximum likelihood estimates for several common probability distributions. These results can be derived by maximizing the corresponding log-likelihood function. \n\n| **Distribution** | **Probability Mass Function, $f(x; \\theta)$** | **Parameter, $\\theta$** | **Maximum Likelihood Estimate, $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Bernoulli | $\\displaystyle \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample |\n| Binomial | $\\displaystyle {N \\choose x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{N-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample |\n| Poisson | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample |\n| Geometric | $\\displaystyle (1-\\theta)^{x-1} \\theta$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$ \n The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Exponential | $\\displaystyle \\theta e^{-\\theta x}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$ \n The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Normal | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\theta_2 \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\theta_1}{\\theta_2} \\right)^2}$ | The mean $(\\theta_1)$ \n\n The standard deviation $(\\theta_2)$ | $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_1 = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ \n The mean of the sample \n\n $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_2 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2}$ \n The standard deviation of the sample |"] [17592186162205 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186161681 :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n$l(\\theta) = 3\\ln\\theta + 4\\ln(1-\\theta)$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ from this sample?"] [17592186161681 :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(3\\ln θ + 4\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{θ} + \\frac{4}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{θ} + \\frac{4}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{θ} + \\frac{4}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(3(θ - 1) + 4θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\n3(θ - 1) + 4θ &= 0 \\\\\n7θ - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n7θ &= 3 \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$"] [17592186161682 :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables $X_i\\sim N(\\theta,1)$ is\n$l(\\theta) = -6\\ln(2\\pi)-8.4 + 12.1\\, \\! \\theta - 6\\ \\! \\theta^2$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ for this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186161682 :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(-6\\ln (2π) - 8.4 + 12.1θ - 6θ^{2}) \\\\\n&= 12.1 - 12θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12.1 - 12θ &= 0 \\\\\n12θ &= 12.1 \\\\\n&= θ \\mid \\approx 1.008\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} \\approx 1.008\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186162395 :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n$l(θ) = 5\\ln θ + \\ln (1 - θ)$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $θ^(^)$ from this sample?"] [17592186163265 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, X_3$ drawn from a population where $X_i\\sim B(5,\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown probability of success. Let's assume we conducted our sample and got the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the log-likelihood function for this sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln 125 + 6 \\ln \\theta +9\\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nOur goal is to form an estimate for the unknown parameter $\\theta$. To do this, we find the value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the probability of getting the observed sample data. In other words, we need to maximize the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$. And, since the natural logarithm is an increasing function, a value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ will also maximize the log-likelihood function $l(\\theta)$.\n\nEstimates of an unknown parameter $\\theta$ obtained by maximizing the likelihood function (or, equivalently, the log-likelihood function) are called **maximum likelihood estimates**. We'll use the usual $\\hat{\\theta}$ notation to denote a maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/00/005367bc78937c5bd5b684cb260aff951de2e85d7c2907bb6e11c312ad4f24bd.png)\n\nTo find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\ln 125 + 6\\ln θ + 9\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(6(θ - 1) + 9θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\n6(θ - 1) + 9θ &= 0 \\\\\n15θ - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n15θ &= 6 \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{6}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{6}{15}\n$$\n\n**Note**: It's easy to check that this is a maximum of $l(\\theta)$ by calculating the second derivative. We'll omit this step."] [17592186163361 :tutorial/content "Let's derive a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimator for the unknown parameter $\\theta$ for a set of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables.\n\nSuppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{n}$ is an I.I.D random sample, where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n}$, the log-likelihood function is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = S\\ln\\theta + (n-S)\\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the number of successes in the sample.\n\nTo compute a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimate, we differentiate $l(\\theta)$, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(S\\ln θ + (n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(S(θ - 1) + (n - S)θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\nS(θ - 1) + (n - S)θ &= 0 \\\\\nSθ - S + nθ - Sθ &= 0 \\\\\nnθ - S &= 0 \\\\\nnθ &= S \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{S}{n} \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nwhich is simply the *mean of the sample*.\n\nThe maximum likelihood estimate for other common distributions can be derived similarly. Let's summarize the most important cases."] [17592186163362 :tutorial/content "The table below summarizes the maximum likelihood estimates for several common probability distributions. These results can be derived by maximizing the corresponding log-likelihood function.\n\n| **Distribution** | **Probability Mass Function, $f(x; \\theta)$** | **Parameter, $\\theta$** | **Maximum Likelihood Estimate, $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Bernoulli | $\\displaystyle \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Binomial | $\\displaystyle {N \\choose x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{N-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Poisson | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Geometric | $\\displaystyle (1-\\theta)^{x-1} \\theta$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$<br>The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Exponential | $\\displaystyle \\theta e^{-\\theta x}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$<br>The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Normal | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\theta_2 \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\theta_1}{\\theta_2} \\right)^2}$ | The mean $(\\theta_1)$<br><br>The standard deviation $(\\theta_2)$ | $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_1 = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample<br><br>$\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_2 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2}$<br>The standard deviation of the sample |"]]}]